// ProblemDetails RFC 9457 problem details (`application/problem+json`). Every
// error response uses this shape. Errors coming from systems
// behind the API are mapped into it and never passed through
// verbatim; i3D.net can retrieve the full detail from its logs
// using the `traceId`.
//
// Stable machine-readable codes (`E` followed by six digits):
//   - E020001: validation failure (422)
//   - E020002: resource not found - unknown UUID, another account's
//     UUID, or a deleted resource (404)
//   - E020003: idempotency conflict - the UUID already denotes a
//     resource created with a different body (409)
//   - E020004: delete blocked - IP addresses are still in use under
//     the network's prefixes (409)
//   - E020005: unknown public fabric identifier - the response lists
//     the currently valid identifiers (422)
//   - E020006: internal or upstream failure, mapped (500)
//   - E020007: refused by network state - the network cannot
//     take a prefix in its current state, which also covers a network
//     whose DELETE has been accepted (its `state` still reads `active`
//     until the teardown succeeds). State-dependent, hence 409: the
//     request may succeed once the network is active, though for an
//     accepted teardown the state it will reach is `deleted`, so the
//     remedy is a different network (409)
//   - E020013: unknown filter field on a listing, or a known filter
//     field with an unsupported operator (400)
//   - E020014: prefix delete blocked - customer IP addresses are
//     still in use under the prefix (409). Distinct from E020004, whose
//     trigger is frozen as the NETWORK-scoped delete-blocked
//     condition - a stable code never changes meaning
//   - E020015: unauthorized - identity not established on the
//     public surface (401)
//   - E020016: quota exceeded - the request would take your account past a
//     configured limit for the resource; the response states the limit,
//     the current usage, and how to request a raise. Nothing is created:
//     no operation is started. State-dependent, so the SAME request
//     succeeds after releasing a resource or having the limit raised -
//     which is why it is 409 and not 422. It is not 403 either: you are
//     allowed to perform the operation, you are simply at your ceiling.
//   - E020017: contract required - self-serve creation is not authorised
//     for this account because no contract covers resources the account
//     provisions for itself. Distinct from E020016 on purpose: an
//     over-limit refusal is fixed by releasing a resource, and this one
